<body>
<h1>Heatmaps with Other Worlds palettes</h1>

<p>Continuous colour maps can look convincing as narrow swatches while losing
structure in small heatmap cells. This vignette tests the Other Worlds maps on
the same centered dataset, where values below and above zero need visibly
different treatment.</p>

<!--begin.rcode setup, echo=FALSE, message=FALSE
knitr::opts_chunk$set(fig.width = 9, fig.height = 3.3, dpi = 120)
library(otherworldspalettes)
z <- scale(volcano)
limit <- max(abs(range(z)))
breaks <- seq(-limit, limit, length.out = 202)
end.rcode-->

<h2>Painting-derived three-colour maps</h2>

<p>These maps favor nuanced, atmospheric transitions. They work well for
larger cells, smooth spatial fields, and figures where colour should sit
quietly behind labels or contours.</p>

<!--begin.rcode soft-heatmaps, echo=TRUE, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.3
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 3), mar = c(1, 1, 2.5, 1))
for (nm in c("solar_tide", "alien_bloom", "cosmic_shore")) {
  image(t(z[nrow(z):1, ]), axes = FALSE, useRaster = TRUE, breaks = breaks,
        col = otherworlds_continuous_palette(201, nm))
  title(nm)
}
par(op)
end.rcode-->

<h2>High-contrast maps</h2>

<p>The high-contrast family uses near-black poles, luminous centers, and
stronger chroma. Choose these for compact cells, screens, or heatmaps where
subtle differences must remain visible at a glance.</p>

<!--begin.rcode high-contrast-heatmaps, echo=TRUE, fig.width=9, fig.height=3.3
op <- par(mfrow = c(1, 3), mar = c(1, 1, 2.5, 1))
for (nm in c("signal_fire", "ultraviolet", "alien_heat")) {
  image(t(z[nrow(z):1, ]), axes = FALSE, useRaster = TRUE, breaks = breaks,
        col = otherworlds_continuous_palette(201, nm))
  title(nm)
}
par(op)
end.rcode-->

<h2>Choosing a map</h2>

<p>Use <code>solar_tide</code> when readers already understand a blue-to-red
temperature metaphor. <code>alien_bloom</code> provides the gentlest
painting-led alternative, while <code>cosmic_shore</code> keeps a cool cyan
center. For maximum separation, <code>signal_fire</code> is the most
conventional, <code>ultraviolet</code> is the most cosmic, and
<code>alien_heat</code> has the strongest Other Worlds identity.</p>

<p>For centered values, use symmetric limits or breaks as above so the
luminous middle colour represents the numerical midpoint. Reverse any map
with <code>direction = -1</code> when the semantic direction of the data calls
for it.</p>
</body>
</html>
